          "type": "turn",
          "question": "possibly nothing but my son ate a peanut bar and now his mouth feels funny and hes making a whistling sound when he breathes in",
          "answer": "**This may be an emergency. Call emergency services now.** Do not wait to see if the symptoms pass, and do not leave your son alone. The urgency rating and action come from a fixed safety rule table. If you have an adrenaline auto-injector and know how to use it, use it now into the outer thigh, following the device's own instructions. You can do this while you call. (Source: red-flag-anaphylaxis, and the urgency rule for anaphylaxis.)\n\nA whistling sound when breathing in, mouth discomfort, and a recent food that may contain peanut can be signs of anaphylaxis, a severe allergic reaction. Anaphylaxis can get worse within minutes. The guidance says to suspect it when symptoms start within minutes to a couple of hours of an exposure. (red-flag-anaphylaxis)\n\n**Signs that make this more urgent, or that mean you should tell the emergency operator straight away:**\n- Trouble breathing, fast breathing, or noisy breathing with a high-pitched sound when breathing in. (red-flag-severe-breathing-difficulty)\n- Skin pulling in between or under the ribs, grunting, or flaring nostrils.\n- Swelling of the lips, tongue, or throat, or a tight throat.\n- Drooling or trouble swallowing.\n- A hoarse voice.\n- Pale or blue-grey lips, tongue, face, or fingertips.\n- Dizziness, faintness, collapse, or unusual drowsiness.\n- Hives, flushing, or widespread swelling.\n- Vomiting or severe stomach pain.\n\n(red-flag-anaphylaxis; red-flag-severe-breathing-difficulty)\n\nStay with your son. Keep him sitting up if he is breathing easily, or lying flat with legs raised if he is faint. Tell the emergency operator what he ate and what symptoms he has. Do not wait for a second opinion before calling.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
